  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $a\\ge b\\ge c\\ge d$ are natural numbers for which\n\n$$ac+ad+bc+bd=10.$$\n\nFind their values.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(a,b,c,d)=(3,2,1,1),(4,1,1,1)$.\n\nFactor as $(a+b)(c+d)=10$ and apply the ordering.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}ac+ad+bc+bd&=a(c+d)+b(c+d)\\\\ac+ad+bc+bd&=(a+b)(c+d).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $(a+b)(c+d)=10$. Since $a+b\\ge c+d\\ge2$, the only possible pair of sums is $(a+b,c+d)=(5,2)$. Hence $c=d=1$. The positive pairs with $a+b=5$ and $a\\ge b\\ge1$ are $(a,b)=(4,1),(3,2)$.\n\nTherefore\n\n$$(a,b,c,d)=(3,2,1,1),(4,1,1,1).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 64
  },
